==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{gloss|obsolete}} [[barbarouse]]

===Etymology===
Late {{inh|en|enm|-}}, from {{der|en|la|barbarus||foreigner, savage}}, from {{der|en|grc|βάρβαρος||foreign, strange}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɑː(ɹ)bəɹəs/}}
{{audio|en|en-us-barbarous.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|said of|_|language}} Not [[classical]] or [[pure]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1880|mainauthor=w:James Redhouse|chapter=Introduction to the second edition|author=Charles Wells|title=Redhouse's Turkish Dictionary|page=vii|passage=The original Turkish tongue was somewhat '''barbarous''', but extremely forcible and concise when spoken.}}
# [[uncivilized|Uncivilized]], [[uncultured]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1801|author=Isaac Watts|title=The improvement of the mind, or A supplement to the art of logic|passage=It is the remark of an ingenious writer, should a '''barbarous''' Indian, who had never seen a palace or a ship, view their separate and disjointed parts, and observe the pillars, doors, windows, cornices and turrets of the one, or the prow and stern, the ribs and masts, the ropes and shrouds, the sails and tackle of the other, he would be able to form but a very lame and dark idea of either of those excellent and useful inventions.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1923|author=Walter de la Mare|title=Seaton's Aunt
|passage=I felt vaguely he was a sneak, and remained quite unmollified by advances on his side, which, in a boy's '''barbarous''' fashion, unless it suited me to be magnanimous, I haughtily ignored.}}
# [[merciless|Mercilessly]] or [[impudently]] [[violent]] or [[cruel]], [[savage]].
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=vi|page=40|passage=Direct my weapon to his '''barbarous''' heart,<br>That thus oppoſeth him againſt the Gods,<br>And ſcornes the Powers that gouerne ''Perſea''.}}
# Like a [[barbarian]], especially in [[sound]]; [[noisy]], [[dissonant]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1673|author=w:John Milton|title=s:I did but prompt the age to quit their cloggs
|passage=I did but prompt the age to quit their cloggs<br>By the known rules of antient libertie,<br>When strait a '''barbarous''' noise environs me<br>Of Owles and Cuckoes, Asses, Apes and Doggs}}

